<body>
<header>
  <h1>Methodology &amp; Caveats</h1>
  <p>Idle/Incremental Game Market Analysis — data pipeline design, known limitations, and confidence ratings.</p>
</header>
<nav>
  <a href="index.html">← Report</a>
</nav>
<main>

  <!-- =========================================================
       DATA SOURCES
  ========================================================= -->
  <section id="sources">
    <h2>Data Sources</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Source</th>
          <th>Role</th>
          <th>Confidence</th>
          <th>Notes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Steam store pages + SteamSpy appdetails</td>
          <td>Primary spine — game metadata, review scores, short descriptions, and real Steam user tags</td>
          <td>High (review % from thousands of reviews); user tags crowd-sourced (medium confidence)</td>
          <td>Per-game Steam user tags are fetched via the SteamSpy appdetails endpoint and used
              for art style, theme, and mechanic classification, supplemented by short description
              keyword matching. Tags are community-applied and can be noisy or popularity-biased.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>SteamSpy</td>
          <td>Owner count estimates</td>
          <td>Medium — modeled estimates, ±30–50% typical</td>
          <td>Not verified sales data. Post-2019 API changes reduced accuracy.
              Treat as order-of-magnitude only.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>itch.io</td>
          <td>Supplementary coverage count</td>
          <td>Low — one browse page, no owner/revenue data</td>
          <td>Free/paid split only. Not included in quantitative charts.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Kongregate</td>
          <td>Supplementary coverage count</td>
          <td>Low — 1–5 star ratings mapped to %; NOT comparable to Steam %</td>
          <td>One page of results. Not included in quantitative charts.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reddit r/incremental_games</td>
          <td>Qualitative sentiment (if available)</td>
          <td>Low / unavailable</td>
          <td>Returns 403 without OAuth. Qualitative only if cached.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- =========================================================
       PIPELINE DESIGN
  ========================================================= -->
  <section id="pipeline">
    <h2>Pipeline Design</h2>
    <h3>Why Steam is the Spine</h3>
    <p>
      Steam is the dominant PC distribution platform for idle games and provides:
      structured game metadata, short descriptions, real Steam user tags (fetched
      via the SteamSpy appdetails endpoint), review counts and percentages, and
      price/business model data. SteamSpy also provides modeled owner counts which,
      despite their uncertainty, are the only freely available proxy for reach on
      the platform.
    </p>
    <h3>Weighted Score Formula</h3>
    <p>
      <code>weighted_score = review_pct_positive × log(1 + total_owners)</code><br>
      This balances quality (review%) with reach (owners). Biases toward popular titles —
      a niche game with 100% reviews will score lower than a popular game with 85%.
      Owner counts use SteamSpy estimates (medium confidence).
    </p>
    <h3>Theme / Mechanic Classification</h3>
    <p>
      Themes, mechanics, and art style are classified from each game's real
      <strong>Steam user tags</strong> (fetched via the SteamSpy appdetails endpoint)
      <em>plus</em> its <strong>short description</strong>, using word-boundary keyword
      matching. Steam user tags are crowd-sourced — community-applied, and can be noisy,
      inconsistent, or popularity-biased — so this is a <strong>medium-confidence</strong>
      heuristic: better than description-only classification, but not authoritative
      genre data. A game may be assigned to multiple themes or mechanics. Results should
      be treated as directional signals, not definitive genre classifications.
    </p>
    <p>
      The "rpg" theme is the most commonly detected in this corpus because "RPG" is
      a very common community-applied Steam tag on idle games. This reflects genuine
      community tagging behaviour on Steam, not a classification artefact.
    </p>
    <h3>Art Style</h3>
    <p>
      Art style is classified from each game's real Steam user tags (e.g. "Pixel Graphics",
      "Anime", "Hand-drawn"). Of 3,479 games, <strong>3,206 have an art style assigned</strong>
      (pixel: 799, hand_drawn: 613, two_d: 516, three_d: 477, minimalist: 438, anime: 235,
      cute: 114, stylized: 14). Tags are crowd-sourced (see caveat above) — medium confidence.
    </p>
    <h3>Underserved Flag (Gaps Module)</h3>
    <p>
      A theme is flagged underserved when:
      <code>supply ≤ 25th percentile AND demand ≥ median demand</code>.
      Thresholds are corpus-relative — adding more games shifts the percentile.
      Treat as directional signal, not a hard market gap.
    </p>
    <h3>Update Cadence</h3>
    <p>
      Active: ≤ 30 days since last detected update.
      Occasional: 31–120 days.
      Abandoned: > 120 days or no update date found.
      Update dates are scraped from Steam — missing dates default to "abandoned".
    </p>
    <h3>Update Cadence Data Source</h3>
    <p>
      Update cadence is derived from the <strong>Steam News API</strong>, which
      is used to detect the most recent news/patch post for 2,169 games.
      A game with no news entry within the last 120 days is classified as
      "abandoned." This is an approximation — news posts do not always correspond
      to gameplay updates.
    </p>
    <h3>Exclusions</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mobile (iOS/Android):</strong> No free/reliable structured data source.</li>
      <li><strong>SteamDB:</strong> Excluded from this pipeline (Cloudflare / ToS barriers).</li>
      <li><strong>Reddit sentiment:</strong> r/incremental_games top.json returns
          403 without OAuth; not collected unless cached data is present.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- =========================================================
       KNOWN LIMITATIONS & CAVEATS
  ========================================================= -->
  <section id="caveats">
    <h2>Known Limitations &amp; Caveats</h2>
    {% if all_caveats %}
    <ul class="caveat-list">
      {% for c in all_caveats %}
      <li>{{ c }}</li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
    {% else %}
    <p>No caveats recorded.</p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  <!-- =========================================================
       CONFIDENCE RATINGS SUMMARY
  ========================================================= -->
  <section id="confidence">
    <h2>Confidence Ratings Summary</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Confidence</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Steam review %</td>
          <td>High</td>
          <td>Direct from Steam; typically thousands of reviews per title.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Owner counts (SteamSpy)</td>
          <td>Medium</td>
          <td>Modeled estimates. ±30–50% error typical. Order-of-magnitude only.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Weighted score</td>
          <td>Medium</td>
          <td>Inherits SteamSpy uncertainty. Useful for ranking, not absolute values.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Theme / mechanic / art style classification</td>
          <td>Medium</td>
          <td>Classified from real Steam user tags (SteamSpy appdetails) plus short description
              keyword matching (word-boundary regex). Tags are crowd-sourced — community-applied,
              can be noisy or popularity-biased. Better than description-only, but not authoritative.
              Art style assigned for 3,206 / 3,479 games.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Underserved flag</td>
          <td>Low–Medium</td>
          <td>Corpus-relative thresholds; sensitive to corpus completeness.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>itch.io coverage</td>
          <td>Low</td>
          <td>One browse page; no owner/revenue data; not comparable to Steam.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Kongregate ratings</td>
          <td>Low</td>
          <td>1–5 star → %; not comparable to Steam review %. One page only.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reddit sentiment</td>
          <td>Low / unavailable</td>
          <td>403 without OAuth; qualitative keyword counts only if cached.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mobile coverage</td>
          <td>Not covered</td>
          <td>No free structured data source available.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

</main>
<footer>
  Idle/Incremental Game Market Analysis — <a href="index.html">Back to Report</a>
</footer>
</body>
</html>
